WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:05.840
Masking up is one of the first things&nbsp;
we did to slow the spread of Covid-19.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:07.280
And it worked.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.760
Study after study has shown that when we&nbsp;
implemented and followed mask mandates cases fell.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.280
But now those mandates are being lifted

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.924
and guidance from public&nbsp;
health experts is changing.

00:00:20.765 --> 00:00:24.080
For many of us, it feels like we’re&nbsp; headed toward 
a new phase in the pandemic

00:00:24.909 --> 00:00:26.269
But it’s not over.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:27.500
Not yet.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.280
So as we weather the third year with Covid-19

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.200
what do we do with the masks?

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.853
The ideal risk mitigation is&nbsp;
to have everyone wearing masks.

00:00:41.853 --> 00:00:45.090
But, of course, we’re not going to wear&nbsp;masks
all the time for the rest of our lives.

00:00:45.597 --> 00:00:46.557
Moving forward...

00:00:46.557 --> 00:00:50.000
It will be left more to&nbsp;individuals 
in most settings

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.720
to decide whether they feel&nbsp;
like they're at higher risk

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.120
or it's a higher risk setting&nbsp;
where they want to wear a mask.

00:00:55.893 --> 00:00:58.320
Fortunately, evidence shows that masks still offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.345
protection even if you’re&nbsp;
the only person wearing one.

00:01:01.345 --> 00:01:06.240
Especially N95 or KN95 types, which have&nbsp;
proven to be exceptionally effective.

00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:09.592
This applies even if you’re&nbsp;
vaccinated and boosted.

00:01:09.592 --> 00:01:13.045
Layering your protection can reduce&nbsp;
the chance of a breakthrough infection.

00:01:13.045 --> 00:01:16.829
What I would hope is that&nbsp;
people would view masks as

00:01:16.829 --> 00:01:21.921
a wonderful, inexpensive medical intervention.

00:01:21.921 --> 00:01:26.160
So when you’re deciding whether or not to mask up, 
consider the weather.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.017
Not the real weather. Pandemic weather.

00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:31.628
Which can rapidly change from week to week.

00:01:31.628 --> 00:01:34.286
If it’s looking bad, you might want to&nbsp;
bring an umbrella 

00:01:34.286 --> 00:01:36.097
as in... wear a mask. 

00:01:36.097 --> 00:01:41.040
We're in an evolving situation. We&nbsp;have been 
since the beginning of 2020.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.629
But there are some principles we can follow here.

00:01:43.629 --> 00:01:47.517
Starting with assessing the case rates&nbsp;
or hospitalizations around the community.

00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:49.719
So looking at cases per 100,000

00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:54.749
you really want it to be at&nbsp;
least under 10 per 100000.

00:01:54.749 --> 00:01:56.465
Ideally in single digits.

00:01:56.465 --> 00:01:58.080
And not just for one day

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.065
but you could look at something 
like the&nbsp;7 or 14 day average for an area.

00:02:02.065 --> 00:02:04.800
Luckily, you can find that&nbsp;
data on a variety of websites

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.240
including the CDC, NY Times, Johns Hopkins&nbsp;
or even by simply searching on Google.

00:02:11.085 --> 00:02:13.245
These numbers will change&nbsp;
depending on the location.

00:02:13.757 --> 00:02:15.037
And also over time.

00:02:15.905 --> 00:02:19.621
There’s some evidence that Covid could&nbsp;
eventually be seasonal, like the flu.

00:02:19.621 --> 00:02:22.400
Which means cases may drop&nbsp;
in the spring or summer.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.720
Only to spike again in the fall and winter.

00:02:25.992 --> 00:02:28.080
Next, it’s important to consider the environment.

00:02:28.785 --> 00:02:31.141
Being outside is a really good start.

00:02:31.141 --> 00:02:37.280
We're heading into spring and I think there will&nbsp;
be less tendency to be indoors in crowded spaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.200
poorly ventilated spaces, for example.

00:02:39.937 --> 00:02:43.781
So that also helps with reduction of transmission.

00:02:43.781 --> 00:02:46.800
But if you’re indoors you should&nbsp;
take a few things into consideration.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.965
Ventilation and the number of people.

00:02:49.965 --> 00:02:54.480
The size of the space and then the&nbsp;
time that you spend in that space

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.320
those are the main factors.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.080
A packed, indoor concert, for example,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.560
is going to be a higher risk&nbsp;
than going to a grocery store&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.897
where the people are more spaced out.

00:03:02.897 --> 00:03:05.881
And events or locations that&nbsp;
are checking vaccination status&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.881 --> 00:03:08.800
or requiring a negative test are even lower risk.

00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:12.918
But even then you need to consider&nbsp;
one other factor...

00:03:13.485 --> 00:03:14.480
yourself.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.335
That includes assessing your own risk of severe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.335 --> 00:03:23.040
disease and hospitalization&nbsp;
and possibly even death.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.080
And that has to do, of course, with&nbsp;
your underlying medical conditions

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.240
whether or not you're immunosuppressed

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.493
whether or not you've been vaccinated and boosted.

00:03:31.493 --> 00:03:33.478
Even if you yourself are low risk 

00:03:33.478 --> 00:03:36.061
you should also&nbsp;&nbsp;consider how dangerous 
an infection&nbsp;would be

00:03:36.061 --> 00:03:37.554
for people closest to you.

00:03:38.317 --> 00:03:41.025
We’ve learned a lot since&nbsp;
the beginning of the pandemic

00:03:41.025 --> 00:03:43.020
and you’ve heard it a thousand times:

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.960
“We’re all in this together.”

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.417
Your actions and decisions will&nbsp;
continue to affect those around you.

00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:51.200
If you are in one of those&nbsp;
lower risk categories and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.940
don't have to wear a mask in all those settings

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.053
understand that if the risk&nbsp;
rises again, you might need to.

00:03:57.053 --> 00:03:59.440
We're not trying to stop every&nbsp;
runny nose in the country.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.920
We're really trying to prevent&nbsp;
people from going to the hospital

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.537
and we're trying to prevent our&nbsp;
health care system from falling apart.

00:04:05.537 --> 00:04:06.581
In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.581 --> 00:04:10.884
be mindful and show some compassion for other&nbsp;
people who may be masking up while you’re not.

00:04:11.529 --> 00:04:13.034
Because your cloudy day 

00:04:13.034 --> 00:04:15.251
may&nbsp;be someone else’s hurricane.

